Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, LIDAM/ISBA, Belgium
Author-Name: Hainaut, Donatien
Author-Email: donatien.hainaut@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, LIDAM/ISBA, Belgium
Author-Name: Hames, Thomas
Author-Email: t.hames@detralytics.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Detralytics
Title: Insurance analytics with clustering techniques
Abstract: The k-means algorithm and its variants are popular clustering techniques. Their purpose is to uncover group structures in a dataset. In actuarial applications, these partitioning methods detect clusters of policies with similar features and allow one to draw up a map of dominant risks. The main challenge lies in de􏰂ning a distance between two observations exclusively characterised by categorical variables. This research paper starts with a review of the k-means algorithm and develops an extension based on Burt's framework to manage categorical rating factors. We then focus on a mini-batch version that keeps computation time under control when analysing a large-scale dataset. We next broaden the scope of application of the fuzzy k-means to fully categorised datasets. Lastly, we conclude with a thorough introduction to spectral clustering and work around the dimensionality issue by reducing the size of the initial dataset with k-means.
Keywords: Clustering analysis ; unsupervised learning ; k-means ; spectral clustering
Length: 27
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, ISBA
Number: 2023002
Creation-Date: 2023-01-12
File-URL: https://dial.uclouvain.be/pr/boreal/en/object/boreal%3A270714/datastream/PDF_01/view
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:aiz:louvad:2023002

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Hainaut, Donatien
Author-Email: donatien.hainaut@uclouvain.be
Author-Workplace-Name: Université catholique de Louvain, LIDAM/ISBA, Belgium
Author-Name: Akbaraly, Adnane
Author-Email: a.akbaraly@detralytics.eu
Author-Workplace-Name: Detralytics
Title: Risk management with Local Least Squares Monte-Carlo
Abstract: The method of least squares Monte-Carlo (LSMC) has become a standard in the insurance and financial sectors for computing the exposure of a company to market risk. The sensitive point of this procedure is the non-linear regression of simulated responses on risk factors. This article proposes a novel approach for this step, based on an a-priori segmentation of responses. Using a K-means algorithm, we identify clusters of responses that are next locally regressed on corresponding risk factors. A global function of regression is obtained by combining local models and a logistic regression. The efficiency of the Local Least squares Monte-Carlo (LLSMC) is checked in two illustrations. The first one focuses on butterfly and bull trap options in a Heston stochastic volatility model. The second illustration analyzes the exposure to risks  of a participating life insurance.

Classification-JEL: C5, G22
Keywords: Least square Monte-Carlo ; risk management ; option valuation
Length: 34
Series: UCLouvain, LIDAM, ISBA
